    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Originalism is rooted in the philosophy of original meaning, primarily advocated by legal scholar Antonin Scalia. Originalists rely on historical legal documents, such as The Federalist Papers, and understandings of the Second Amendment during the 18th century. For instance, they may reference the intentions behind the 1789 Militia Act and historical accounts of private militias' importance to early states' defense. When this perspective was applied in the Supreme Court, Crawford v. Marion County Election Board (2008) struck down voter ID laws but was later paralleled in United States v. Marquis (1981) upholding a constitutionally compliant rifle permit.\n\nLiving constitutionalists, conversely, draw on theories of dynamic interpretation, such as the one articulated by legal scholar Ronald Dworkin. This approach takes into account societal changes, including mental health research, urbanization, and modern firearm technology. In the landmark case District of Columbia v. Heller (2008), Justice Scalia, writing for the majority, initially struck down gun control laws for an individual while still finding room within modern conceptions of a \"well-regulated militia.\" The dissent, penned by Justice Stevens, reflects living constitutional thought, arguing that the lack of a militia threat means",
      "source": "target",
